            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "A straight-line imitation of the sigmoid: 0 below -3, 1 above 3, and a straight ramp from 0 to 1 in between (the input divided by 6, plus 0.5).\n\nIt is cheaper than the sigmoid (no exponential) and reaches its limits exactly, rather than only approaching them. As a shape in a patch it maps -3..3 onto 0..1 and holds everything outside.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 gives 0.5. Each unit of input moves the output by one sixth. At -3 and 3 the ramp meets its two flat stretches with a sharp corner each - the corners a real sigmoid does not have.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.hardsigmoid\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -6 (left edge of the plot) to 6 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -1.5 to 1.5; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.sigmoid is the smooth original. t.nn.hardswish multiplies the input by this. t.nn.hardtanh is the same flat-ramp-flat shape from -1 to 1 with limits you set. t.nn.relu6 is the same shape again, from 0 to 6.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 690,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "A straight-line imitation of the sigmoid: 0 below -3, 1 above 3, and a straight ramp from 0 to 1 in between (the input divided by 6, plus 0.5).\n\nIt is cheaper than the sigmoid (no exponential) and reaches its limits exactly, rather than only approaching them. As a shape in a patch it maps -3..3 onto 0..1 and holds everything outside.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 gives 0.5. Each unit of input moves the output by one sixth. At -3 and 3 the ramp meets its two flat stretches with a sharp corner each - the corners a real sigmoid does not have.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.hardsigmoid\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -6 (left edge of the plot) to 6 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -1.5 to 1.5; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.sigmoid is the smooth original. t.nn.hardswish multiplies the input by this. t.nn.hardtanh is the same flat-ramp-flat shape from -1 to 1 with limits you set. t.nn.relu6 is the same shape again, from 0 to 6."
        },
